      "ask_text": "Hello — I need a copy of the product list from the system we use in the shop now, so the new app starts with our real range instead of the made-up examples I've been building against.\n\nWhatever the system can export is fine — a spreadsheet, a CSV, even a printout if that's all it does. What matters is that it's the full list and it's current.\n\nIf it lets you choose which columns to include, could you tick everything? I'm particularly hoping it has HSN codes and tax rates already, because if it does it saves the accountant a lot of work — but prices, costs, barcodes and pack sizes are all useful too.\n\nAlso, could you tell me what the system is called? It helps me know what to expect from the file.\n\nNo rush. I'd just rather build against the real list than keep guessing. Thank you.",
      "body": "**Who:** Owner.\n\nRaised 2026-08-18, when it became clear this repository had never recorded where the real catalogue comes from. The 60 products in the prototypes are demo data invented to build screens against; the shop's actual range is whatever the current system holds, and **nobody here has seen it.**\n\n## What this unblocks, and what it does not\n\n**Unblocks:** the HSN question, which currently asks the accountant to supply a code for every product. If the export already carries them, that becomes *\"please check these\"* instead — a much smaller favour and far likelier to come back. It also gives the GST-scheme question a real product mix to ask about rather than a guess, and it is the input to the Phase 2 catalogue import.\n\n**Does not resolve:** whether any HSN codes in the export are *correct*, the GST scheme, or the rounding rule. Those still need the accountant. This makes their job smaller and their answers checkable; it does not do it for them.\n\n## What to look for when it arrives\n\nBefore anything is imported, read the export and record what it actually has, because each of these changes downstream work:\n\n- **HSN codes and tax rates** — if present, `accountant-hsn-values.md` becomes a review rather than an authoring job.\n- **Cost prices** — `admin.md` §7 says cost enters only through purchasing and test case 1.31 guards it. Imported costs are an opening balance, not a purchase, and need an honest story: either a synthetic opening goods receipt, or costs left empty until the first real receipt. **Do not quietly write them onto product records** — that is precisely the second source of truth 1.31 exists to prevent.\n- **Product identity** — whatever key the current system uses. The design settled on an opaque id plus a unique slug specifically so identity is never authored twice, and an import is exactly where a second authoring happens. The old key should be kept as a reference field, not adopted as the id.\n- **Variants** — whether a 1 kg and a 5 kg pack are separate rows or one row with sizes. `admin.md` §6 requires separate rows, and test case 1.30 is about what goes wrong when they are not.\n- **Weighed goods** — how loose items are represented, and whether price-per-kg is carried.\n- **Barcodes** — zero, one or many per product; *no barcode* is a valid state and the norm for loose goods.\n- **How many products there actually are.** Every \"60 products\" sentence in this repository is fixture data. The real number is unknown.\n\n## Format does not matter much\n\nCSV, Excel, a printout, or a database dump are all workable. What matters is that it is **complete and current** — a partial export would seed a catalogue that quietly disagrees with the shop, which is worse than an empty one.\n\n## Where it stands\n\nNot asked.\n\n*Append dated lines. When the export arrives, record what fields it actually carried — that answer is the thing several other records are waiting on.*",
